\description{
This function estimates the home range of each track using autocorrelated
kernel density estimation (AKDE, Fleming et al. 2015), as implemented in
the package \code{ctmm} (\code{\link[ctmm:akde]{ctmm::akde()}}). Unlike conventional kernel density
estimation (\code{\link[=hr_tt_kde]{hr_tt_kde()}}), AKDE accounts for the autocorrelation of
locations in tracking data, which otherwise leads to underestimating home
ranges. AKDE requires a movement model for each track, which must first be
fitted with \code{\link[=ctmm_tt_fit]{ctmm_tt_fit()}}.
}
\details{
The utilisation distributions (UDs) are estimated on a grid
shared by all groups (unless a \code{bbox} is given for each group), so that
they can be combined or compared (e.g. with \code{\link[=hr_tt_ud_sum]{hr_tt_ud_sum()}} or
\code{\link[=hr_tt_ud_overlap]{hr_tt_ud_overlap()}}). By default, the grid follows the defaults of
\code{\link[ctmm:akde]{ctmm::akde()}}: its resolution is a tenth of the smallest bandwidth (or
of the smallest standard deviation of the home range of the movement
model) among all groups, and it extends far enough from the locations to
contain all but a fraction \code{tolerance} of each UD. The cells are square,
so that the grid can be used with the other \code{hr_tt_} functions.

Each UD is estimated by \code{ctmm} directly on the grid (i.e. it is not
resampled), and is standardised to sum to 1 over the grid. The \code{ctmm} UD
objects are also returned (in column \code{ud_ctmm}), so that they can be used
with other functions of \code{ctmm} (e.g. \code{\link[ctmm:overlap]{ctmm::overlap()}} or
\code{\link[ctmm:meta]{ctmm::meta()}}).

The models are matched to the tracks by track id. As models are fitted to
each track, each group of \code{x} must contain a single track (which is the
case when \code{x} is not grouped). The tracks must not have changed since the
models were fitted (i.e. they must have the same events with a location).
Models without a home range (e.g. Brownian motion) cannot be used.
}
